# Shared by deploy/Caddyfile (production) and deploy/Caddyfile.local (the
# no-TLS localhost harness), and shared ON PURPOSE. The local smoke run exists
# to prove that streaming survives the proxy; if it exercised a different
# proxy configuration than production, it would prove nothing about production.
# Both files import this one, so there is exactly one place where the streaming
# behaviour is defined.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(ohcamel_common) {
	# The stream is excluded from compression. gzip works by accumulating bytes
	# before emitting a block, which is the wrong shape for a stream -- though
	# Caddy's encode module turns out to handle text/event-stream correctly on
	# its own (see the note below the header block; it was tested). The
	# exclusion stays because it costs nothing and is right against a less
	# careful proxy. Every other route is a discrete response and compresses
	# fine; the dashboard HTML is the one that actually benefits.
	@compressible not path /api/stream
	encode @compressible zstd gzip

	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy no-referrer
		-Server
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Streaming, and an honest account of what these two directives are worth.
#
# The fear these were written against: a reverse proxy that buffers the SSE
# body produces a dashboard that loads, renders one snapshot from
# /api/snapshot, and then freezes. It looks exactly like an engine bug, and it
# would make the project's reactive claim unfalsifiable from outside precisely
# where it is most visible.
#
# That fear turns out to be largely unfounded on Caddy, and it was worth
# finding out rather than assuming. Three deliberate misconfigurations were
# tested against this stack, each with the smoke suite watching:
#
#   flush_interval 30s   -- longer than the whole probe window.  Still streamed.
#   encode applied to /api/stream (gzip requested by the client). Still streamed.
#   nginx with proxy_buffering on, in Caddy's place.              Still streamed.
#
# Caddy special-cases responses whose Content-Type is text/event-stream and
# flushes them immediately regardless of flush_interval; its encode module does
# the same. So these directives are defence in depth, not the thing holding the
# dashboard up.
#
# They are kept anyway, for two narrow reasons. The auto-detection keys off the
# Content-Type, so a future route that streams something which is not
# text/event-stream would get no such protection. And an explicit -1 documents
# the requirement for whoever swaps this proxy for one that is less careful --
# which is a real possibility, and the reason the smoke suite tests the
# behaviour from outside rather than trusting any of these lines.
#
# The compression exclusion below is kept on the same footing: verified
# unnecessary here, cheap, and correct against a proxy that is less thoughtful.
(ohcamel_stream_proxy) {
	flush_interval -1
	transport http {
		read_timeout 0
		write_timeout 0
	}
}
